media: verisilicon: h713-av1: move narrow frames to the buffer's pitch

The H713 AV1 core writes its NV12/P010 output at a pitch of its own,
the frame width rounded up to 64 (samples, two bytes each at 10 bits),
and ignores the stride fields its register map has: writing the
capture buffer's bytesperline, 64 or 2048 into LU/CB/CR_STRIDE left the
output identical. That is right for a stream whose frames fill the
buffer, and wrong for any frame narrower than it: an AV1 frame-size
change (frame_size_override, libaom's resize mode) leaves the buffer at
the sequence's maximum size, and a 1024-wide frame written 1024 bytes
per row into a buffer read at 1280 comes out sheared from row 1.

Record, at frame setup, the pitch the core will write at when it is
smaller than the buffer's, and move the rows to the buffer's pitch in
a new vb2 buf_finish hook, in process context before the buffer
reaches userspace: luma from the top of the plane, chroma from the
chroma base the core was given, last row first so that no row is
overwritten before it is moved. The NV12 output is the core's
secondary output; its references live in buffers of their own, so the
move cannot disturb later frames. The variant keeps a kernel mapping
of its CAPTURE buffers for this (capture_kmap); every other variant and
codec leaves restride_pitch at 0 and is unchanged.

Measured 2026-10-04 on resize-fixed / resize-random through FFmpeg
VA-API and GStreamer v4l2slav1dec: frame 0, sheared before, is now
bit-exact vs libdav1d, as is every key frame and every inter frame
whose references have its own size. Inter frames that predict from a
reference of another size (AV1 reference scaling) are still wrong; that
is separate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
